<td id="S5.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">LUT-2D</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">High (<math id="S5.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{2B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{2B}</annotation></semantics></math> entries)</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">Highest</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">Long (1.9x)</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">LUT-1D</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">Medium (<math id="S5.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> entries)</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">High</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">Medium (1.2x)</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.5" class="ltx_td" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">STE</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">Low (No LUT)</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">Low</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;">Short (1.0x)</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_border_bb" style="padding-left:5.0pt;padding-right:5.0pt;"></td></tr>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="S5.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">+</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Detailed retraining accuracy results are reported in Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Experimental Results ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a></span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">*</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Retraining runtime was profiled on CIFAR-10 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> with the VGG19 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> model using a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPU and batch size of 64.
The applied AppMult is mult8u_1DMU from the EvoApproxLib <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> library.</p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LUT-2D method uses a 2D LUT,
denoted as <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}[W,X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad2D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}[W,X]</annotation></semantics></math>,
to store the gradient <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}}{\partial X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}}{\partial X}</annotation></semantics></math> at each integer input pair <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This method characterizes the gradient in a fine-grained way,
capturing variations in the changing rate of the AppMult function <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{AM}}(W,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{AM}}(W,X)</annotation></semantics></math> near different inputs <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For a <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>-bit AppMult,
<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}[W,X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad2D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}[W,X]</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{2B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{2B}</annotation></semantics></math> entries,
each corresponding to a unique pair <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,X)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For clarity,
we illustrate the LUT-2D method using unsigned AppMults,
though it extends naturally to signed cases.
The 2D gradient LUT is computed in two steps:
1) smoothing the AppMult function, and
2) computing a difference-based gradient.
Sections <a href="#S5.SS2.SSS1" title="V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B1</span></a> and <a href="#S5.SS2.SSS2" title="V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B2</span></a> describe these steps,
and Section <a href="#S5.SS2.SSS3" title="V-B3 Backward Propagation with 2D Gradient LUTs ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B3</span></a> explains the CUDA kernel implementation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span>1 </span>Smoothing the AppMult Function</h4>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="" id="S5.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_missing ltx_missing_image" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The AppMult function,
its smoothed AppMult function using a half window size of 4,
and the AccMult function when <math id="S5.F4.sf1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{f}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{f}=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="" id="S5.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_missing ltx_missing_image" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S5.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">LUT-2D, LUT-1D, and STE-based gradients of the AppMult when <math id="S5.F4.sf2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{f}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{f}=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 4: </span>Smoothing of a 7-bit unsigned AppMult function <math id="S5.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AM(W_{f}=10,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AM(W_{f}=10,X)</annotation></semantics></math> and its gradient estimations.
The AppMult corresponds to Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ III-A Approximate Multipliers (AppMults) ‣ III Preliminaries ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>,
which removes the 6 rightmost columns of partial products.
The red arrows show three large changes in the AppMult function,
corresponding to the large values in the LUT-2D gradient.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To compute <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}}{\partial X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}}{\partial X}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we analyze <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is a fixed integer operand.
Since AppMults often approximate or remove least significant partial products,
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X)</annotation></semantics></math> typically exhibit a stair-like pattern <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w.r.t.</span> <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>
(see the black curve in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4.sf1" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>).
As a result,
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}}{\partial X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}}{\partial X}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero for most <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">exhibits</span> large values at the stair edges.
This behavior is problematic for gradient descent,
because zero gradients prohibit parameter updates,
while spikes at edges can destabilize the gradient descent process.
Thus, directly using the true gradient leads to poor retraining results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We address this by smoothing <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X)</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(W_{f},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(W_{f},X)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">via local averaging</span>:</p>
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S(W_{f},X)=\frac{1}{2{\textit{HWS}}+1}\sum_{\Delta x=-{\textit{HWS}}}^{{\textit{HWS}}}{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X+\Delta x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S(W_{f},X)=\frac{1}{2{\textit{HWS}}+1}\sum_{\Delta x=-{\textit{HWS}}}^{{\textit{HWS}}}{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X+\Delta x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{for }{\textit{HWS}}\leq X\leq 2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{for }{\textit{HWS}}\leq X\leq 2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the bitwidth and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HWS</span> is a positive integer called <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">half window size</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For each input pair <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W_{f},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W_{f},X)</annotation></semantics></math>,
Eq. (<a href="#S5.E8" title="In V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) considers its neighbor points within a window of size <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2{\textit{HWS}}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2{\textit{HWS}}+1)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and calculates the average of the AppMult outputs in this window to produce the smoothed output <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(W_{f},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(W_{f},X)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For example, the red curve in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4.sf1" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> shows the smoothed function <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(W_{f}=10,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(W_{f}=10,X)</annotation></semantics></math>
for the AppMult function <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{AM}}(W_{f}=10,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{AM}}(W_{f}=10,X)</annotation></semantics></math> (shown in blue) with <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{HWS}}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{HWS}}=4</annotation></semantics></math>.
After smoothing, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(W_{f}=10,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(W_{f}=10,X)</annotation></semantics></math> has no zero gradients and no large gradients,
making it more suitable for gradient descent.
Note that Eq. (<a href="#S5.E8" title="In V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) is only applied to <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{HWS}}\leq X\leq 2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext><mo>≤</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{HWS}}\leq X\leq 2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is because only <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(W_{f},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(W_{f},X)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{HWS}}\leq X\leq 2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext><mo>≤</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{HWS}}\leq X\leq 2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}</annotation></semantics></math> is used in the LUT-2D gradient estimation,
which will be shown in Section <a href="#S5.SS2.SSS2" title="V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span>2 </span>Difference-Based Gradient Computation</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">After smoothing,
the 2D gradient LUT is computed using the following difference-based method:</p>
<table id="S5.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\displaystyle\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}[W_{f},X]=\frac{S(W_{f},X)}{\partial X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad2D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\displaystyle\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}[W_{f},X]=\frac{S(W_{f},X)}{\partial X}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E9Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{S(W_{f},X+1)-S(W_{f},X-1)}{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{S(W_{f},X+1)-S(W_{f},X-1)}{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E9Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E9Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{for }{\textit{HWS}}&lt;X&lt;2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{for }{\textit{HWS}}&lt;X&lt;2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Eq. (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) considers the two neighboring points of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W_{f},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W_{f},X)</annotation></semantics></math>,
<span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W_{f},X+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W_{f},X+1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W_{f},X-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W_{f},X-1)</annotation></semantics></math>,
and uses the slope between them to approximate the gradient.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that Eq. (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) is only applied to <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{HWS}}&lt;X&lt;2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{HWS}}&lt;X&lt;2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the other values of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
we approximate the gradient as</p>
<table id="S5.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\displaystyle\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}[W_{f},X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad2D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\displaystyle\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}[W_{f},X]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E10Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E10Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E10Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\max\limits_{0\leq X^{\prime}&lt;2^{B}}AM(W,X^{\prime})-\min\limits_{0\leq X^{\prime}&lt;2^{B}}AM(W,X^{\prime})}{2^{B}-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\max\limits_{0\leq X^{\prime}&lt;2^{B}}AM(W,X^{\prime})-\min\limits_{0\leq X^{\prime}&lt;2^{B}}AM(W,X^{\prime})}{2^{B}-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E10Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E10Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{for }0\leq X\leq{\textit{HWS}}\text{ and }2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}\leq X&lt;2^{B}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext><mtext> and </mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{for }0\leq X\leq{\textit{HWS}}\text{ and }2^{B}-1-{\textit{HWS}}\leq X&lt;2^{B}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Eq. (<a href="#S5.E10" title="In V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) computes the maximum and minimum values of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AM(W_{f},X^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AM(W_{f},X^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}\in[0,2^{B}-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}\in[0,2^{B}-1]</annotation></semantics></math>,
which is the total change of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AM(W_{f},X^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AM(W_{f},X^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> for all possible values of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> under the fixed <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This total change is then divided by
<math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2^{B}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2^{B}-1)</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain the average change per unit <math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as the gradient approximation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Using Eqs. (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) and (<a href="#S5.E10" title="In V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>)
with <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{f}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{f}=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{HWS}}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">HWS</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{HWS}}=4</annotation></semantics></math>,
the LUT-2D gradient is shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4.sf2" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a> in red.
For comparison,
the STE-based gradient is plotted in green in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4.sf2" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>,
showing a constant value of 10 for all values of <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{f}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{f}=10</annotation></semantics></math>.
The LUT-2D gradient reflects the varing changing rate in <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X)</annotation></semantics></math> w.r.t. <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,
where larger gradient values indicate greater changes of <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{AM}}(W_{f},X)</annotation></semantics></math> per unit <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Note that <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{AM}}(W_{f}=10,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{AM}}(W_{f}=10,X)</annotation></semantics></math> has three relatively large changes at <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=31</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="63" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>63</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">63</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="Thmexample1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">95</annotation></semantics></math>,
indicated by the red arrows in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4.sf1" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>.
Correspondingly, the LUT-2D gradient in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4.sf2" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a> exhibits large values near these points.
In contrast, the STE method misses these variations,
providing less informative guidance for gradient descent.
Thus, the LUT-2D method can lead to higher retraining accuracy.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span>3 </span>Backward Propagation with 2D Gradient LUTs</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In deep learning models,
whether NNs or a transformers,
the main operation that uses AppMults is GEMM.
During backward propagation, we therefore need the backward pass of approximate GEMM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bf Y={\textit{AppGEMM(\bf W, \bf X)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AppGEMM(</mtext><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">W, X)</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bf Y={\textit{AppGEMM(\bf W, \bf X)}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where the integer weight matrix <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bf W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bf W</annotation></semantics></math> has size <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times K</annotation></semantics></math>,
the integer activation matrix <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bf X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bf X</annotation></semantics></math> has size <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\times N</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the integer output matrix <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bf Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bf Y</annotation></semantics></math> has size <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\times N</annotation></semantics></math>,
with entries</p>
<table id="S5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{ij}=\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}{\textit{AM}}(W_{ik},X_{kj}),0\leq i&lt;M,0\leq j&lt;N." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{ij}=\sum_{k=0}^{K-1}{\textit{AM}}(W_{ik},X_{kj}),0\leq i&lt;M,0\leq j&lt;N.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Given the gradient of the loss function <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w.r.t.</span> <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>,
denoted <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial L}{\partial Y_{ij}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial L}{\partial Y_{ij}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the gradient of <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w.r.t.</span> <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{kj}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{kj}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S5.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial L}{\partial X_{kj}}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial L}{\partial X_{kj}}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E12X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{M-1}\frac{\partial L}{\partial Y_{ij}}\times\frac{\partial Y_{ij}}{\partial X_{kj}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{M-1}\frac{\partial L}{\partial Y_{ij}}\times\frac{\partial Y_{ij}}{\partial X_{kj}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E12Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E12Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E12Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{M-1}\frac{\partial L}{\partial Y_{ij}}\times\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}(W_{ik},X_{kj})}{\partial X_{kj}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=0}^{M-1}\frac{\partial L}{\partial Y_{ij}}\times\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}(W_{ik},X_{kj})}{\partial X_{kj}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E12Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E12Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{for }0\leq k&lt;K,0\leq j&lt;N." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{for }0\leq k&lt;K,0\leq j&lt;N.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">We store the AppMult gradient in a 2D LUT
and compute <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}(W_{ik},X_{kj})}{\partial X_{kj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}(W_{ik},X_{kj})}{\partial X_{kj}}</annotation></semantics></math> by reading the LUT entry <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}[W_{ik},X_{kj}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad2D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}[W_{ik},X_{kj}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We implement a CUDA kernel for this backward pass.
Specifically,
we precompute the AppMult gradients <span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w.r.t.</span> <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for all integer pairs <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,X)</annotation></semantics></math> using Eqs. (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) and (<a href="#S5.E10" title="In V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) and store them in the 2D LUT <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad2D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{Grad2D}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The precomputation of the AppMult gradient <span id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">w.r.t.</span> <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> can be done similarly,
resulting in another 2D LUT <math id="S5.SS2.SSS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{Grad2D}}_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad2D</mtext><mi>W</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{Grad2D}}_{W}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The CUDA kernel receives these 2D gradient LUTs and calculates Eq. (<a href="#S5.E12" title="In V-B3 Backward Propagation with 2D Gradient LUTs ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>).
Since AppMults of up to 8 bits are considered,
the LUT sizes are small enough to fit in GPU memory,
making the approach efficient for retraining.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LUT-1D Method: Memory- and Runtime-Efficient AppMult Gradients</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A drawback of the LUT-2D method is its large table size of <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{2B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{2B}</annotation></semantics></math> entries.
Such large LUTs slow down gradient lookups.
To reduce memory and runtime,
we construct a more compact 1D LUT.
We first describe its construction and
then explain the backward propagation using the 1D LUT.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span>1 </span>1D Gradient LUT Construction</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We use a 1D LUT, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{Grad1D}}_{X}[W]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad1D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{Grad1D}}_{X}[W]</annotation></semantics></math>,
to store <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}}{\partial X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}}{\partial X}</annotation></semantics></math> for each integer <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{Grad1D}}_{X}[W]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad1D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{Grad1D}}_{X}[W]</annotation></semantics></math> depends only on <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> and is independent of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{Grad1D}}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad1D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{Grad1D}}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> contains <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> entries,
one for each possible integer <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each entry is computed as</p>
<table id="S5.E13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E13X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E13X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\displaystyle\textit{Grad1D}}_{X}[W]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad1D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\displaystyle\textit{Grad1D}}_{X}[W]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.E13Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E13Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E13Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\max\limits_{0\leq X^{\prime}&lt;2^{B}}AM(W,X^{\prime})-\min\limits_{0\leq X^{\prime}&lt;2^{B}}AM(W,X^{\prime})}{2^{B}-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\max\limits_{0\leq X^{\prime}&lt;2^{B}}AM(W,X^{\prime})-\min\limits_{0\leq X^{\prime}&lt;2^{B}}AM(W,X^{\prime})}{2^{B}-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This corresponds to the average changing rate of <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="AM(W,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">AM(W,X)</annotation></semantics></math> acroos all possible <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> values at a fixed <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The LUT-1D method can be viewed as a special case of the LUT-2D method.
When the HWS value in the LUT-2D method is <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{B-1}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{B-1}-1</annotation></semantics></math>,
Eq. (<a href="#S5.E9" title="In V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) is never used,
and Eq. (<a href="#S5.E10" title="In V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) applies for all <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> at a fixed <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this case,
the LUT-2D method reduces exactly to the LUT-1D method.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmexample2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmexample2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmexample2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmexample2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmexample2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the same AppMult function <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{AM}}(W_{f}=10,X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{AM}}(W_{f}=10,X)</annotation></semantics></math> as in Example <a href="#Thmexample1" title="Example 1 ‣ V-B2 Difference-Based Gradient Computation ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
The LUT-1D gradient from Eq. (<a href="#S5.E13" title="In V-C1 1D Gradient LUT Construction ‣ V-C LUT-1D Method: Memory- and Runtime-Efficient AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) is shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4.sf2" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a> with the blue line.
Comparing with the STE-based gradient in green,
which is a constant 10,
the LUT-1D method yieads a smaller gradient value of 9.07.
This is reasonable since the AppMult function in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4.sf1" title="In Fig. 4 ‣ V-B1 Smoothing the AppMult Function ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a> grows more slowly than the AccMult function,
whose gradient equals 10 when <math id="Thmexample2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{f}=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{f}=10</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although the LUT-1D method does not capture fine-grained variations as the LUT-2D method does,
it reduces memory and lookup time.
It also provides a more precise gradient estimation than STE,
potentially improving retraining accuracy.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span>2 </span>Backward Propagation with 1D Gradient LUTs</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The backward propagation for AppGEMM with the 1D gradient LUTs follows the procedure in Section <a href="#S5.SS2.SSS3" title="V-B3 Backward Propagation with 2D Gradient LUTs ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V-B3</span></a>.
The only difference is that in Eq. (<a href="#S5.E12" title="In V-B3 Backward Propagation with 2D Gradient LUTs ‣ V-B LUT-2D Method: High-Accuracy AppMult Gradients ‣ V Gradient Estimation of AppMults for High-Accuracy Retraining ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>),
the gradient term
<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}(W_{ik},X_{kj})}{\partial X_{kj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">AM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial{\textit{AM}}(W_{ik},X_{kj})}{\partial X_{kj}}</annotation></semantics></math>
is obtained from the 1D LUT entry <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\textit{Grad1D}}_{X}[W_{ik}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">Grad1D</mtext><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\textit{Grad1D}}_{X}[W_{ik}]</annotation></semantics></math>,
instead of the 2D LUT entry.
Since the 1D LUT has only has <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>B</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> entries,
gradient lookups are much faster than with the <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{2B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{2B}</annotation></semantics></math>-entry 2D LUT,
leading to shorter retraining runtime.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Results</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implemented our AppMult-aware retraining framework in PyTorch 2.4 and CUDA 12.4.
The framework supports AppMults with bit-widths up to 8 and allows user-defined gradients.
Since 8-bit and sub-8-bit arithmetic are commonly used in low-power deep learning accelerators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>,
we do not study AppMults beyond 8 bits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We first report the hardware cost of the AppMults used in our experiments (Section <a href="#S6.SS1" title="VI-A Hardware Cost of AppMults ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-A</span></a>).
We then present the AppMult-aware retraining results on deep learning models,
covering CNNs (Section <a href="#S6.SS2" title="VI-B Experiments on AppMult-Based CNNs and CIFAR-10/100 Dataset ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-B</span></a>) and vision transformers (Section <a href="#S6.SS3" title="VI-C Experiments on AppMult-Based Vision Transformers and ImageNet Dataset ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-C</span></a>).
Finally, we evaluate the effectiveness of the proposed gradient estimation methods under low-bit quantization (Section <a href="#S6.SS4" title="VI-D Exploration on Extremely Low-Bit AppMults ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Gradient Estimation Methods of Approximate Multipliers for High-Accuracy Retraining of Deep Learning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI-D</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hardware Cost of AppMults</span></h3>

<figure id="S6.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE II: </span>Information of tested multipliers,
including area, delay, power, and error metrics.
The term “_<span id="S6.T2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">rmk</span>” indicates the removal of the rightmost <math id="S6.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> columns of partial products.
The term “_<span id="S6.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic">syn</span>” means the AppMult is generated by the approximate logic synthesis tool <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>.</figcaption>
